{"edition_id":"urn:uuid:35b4350d-7202-5d47-8b85-5262d7ca441c","id":"urn:uuid:be15e65b-78ec-5858-b1b9-5b6991b7e162","order":3,"protected_tokens":[],"record_type":"segment","rights_id":"urn:uuid:52a7c815-5b13-5f21-a52a-e7fa11238e07","schema_id":"ttna-segment-v1","schema_version":"1.0.0","semantic_slot":"paragraph","source_block_sha256":"14adeb9579bf809ac94ccf671148b3650f1d78ce56438f6ab15aaba2ada54a51","source_file_id":"urn:uuid:271e812b-1ccd-53ed-a402-07fe58709c8a","source_locale":"en-US","source_locator":{"end_line":690,"layout":"Standard","layout_ordinal":37,"start_line":673},"source_text":"I gratefully acknowledge the generous support I received during the writing of this textbook, from the patience my immediate family, Amy, Cecelia, and Victorija exercised while I was absorbed by my laptop's screen, to the willingness of my Spring 2013 Seminar class, Elizabeth Field, Rachael Ivison, Amanda Reyher, and Steven Warner to read and criticize an early version of the first chapter. The Woodbridge Public Library staff, especially Pamela Wilonski, helped provide a peaceful and inspirational environment for writing the bulk of the first edition text. Many thanks to Dick Pelosi for his extensive review and many kind words and encouragements throughout the endeavor to create the first edition. I also owe a hearty thanks to the folks at CollegeOpenTextbooks.org, who provided unpaid copy editing. Finally, many thanks to the users who have provided corrections and suggestions for improvements. A number of them have found their way into the third edition.","source_text_sha256":"7ee12e07f67e077be5e9b1a29db6ec944845ad9ab57921e2620747897ab80937","translation_state":"source_frozen","unit_id":"urn:uuid:ba0206aa-b20f-54ce-b69e-4fdf460ce7dc"}
{"edition_id":"urn:uuid:35b4350d-7202-5d47-8b85-5262d7ca441c","id":"urn:uuid:c3962fe6-e0f4-537d-8b11-cd44ff2e4199","order":1,"protected_tokens":[],"record_type":"segment","rights_id":"urn:uuid:52a7c815-5b13-5f21-a52a-e7fa11238e07","schema_id":"ttna-segment-v1","schema_version":"1.0.0","semantic_slot":"title","source_block_sha256":"17c7659280830b34af5e797acab5d46df56f393fefbf375ced7ce46290dee31e","source_file_id":"urn:uuid:271e812b-1ccd-53ed-a402-07fe58709c8a","source_locale":"en-US","source_locator":{"end_line":446,"layout":"Subsubsection*","layout_ordinal":15,"start_line":444},"source_text":"Online (in the cloud)","source_text_sha256":"45a8242bf82b099b349c0810983f46211b44a81aa916289d608d5b5293deb93c","translation_state":"source_frozen","unit_id":"urn:uuid:309948d3-a03e-5749-a53b-6f5dcf605717"}
{"edition_id":"urn:uuid:35b4350d-7202-5d47-8b85-5262d7ca441c","id":"urn:uuid:cd1ecff9-b8b9-533d-bd66-f6e9ee4e52be","order":2,"protected_tokens":[],"record_type":"segment","rights_id":"urn:uuid:52a7c815-5b13-5f21-a52a-e7fa11238e07","schema_id":"ttna-segment-v1","schema_version":"1.0.0","semantic_slot":"paragraph","source_block_sha256":"9c07b70cbdaa9eb5ed199c6c5f597ecbaa122a6b65296f319cf78dc485fb98f8","source_file_id":"urn:uuid:271e812b-1ccd-53ed-a402-07fe58709c8a","source_locale":"en-US","source_locator":{"end_line":455,"layout":"Standard","layout_ordinal":16,"start_line":448},"source_text":"Perhaps the easiest way to get started using Octave is through a web browser. This approach has the advantage of quick startup, no installation, and very modest hardware requirements. Online Octave environments can be run on the most powerful workstations or on a phone. As of this writing, there are at least two free online platforms available.","source_text_sha256":"262650a648871ee8a98a1f08f5fb334c3a6edda46b579caeeaac78a69b7d0313","translation_state":"source_frozen","unit_id":"urn:uuid:309948d3-a03e-5749-a53b-6f5dcf605717"}
{"edition_id":"urn:uuid:35b4350d-7202-5d47-8b85-5262d7ca441c","id":"urn:uuid:d6082002-3120-51f6-a414-ba0b6d6ec8ae","order":6,"protected_tokens":[{"command_kind":"href","header":"CommandInset href","kind":"CommandInset","ordinal":1,"sha256":"aefb7b2dac0ade048c8605d0545131afab7edd4a7a47fa4e6caddbefd9025994","target":"https://www.gnu.org/software/octave/"}],"record_type":"segment","rights_id":"urn:uuid:52a7c815-5b13-5f21-a52a-e7fa11238e07","schema_id":"ttna-segment-v1","schema_version":"1.0.0","semantic_slot":"paragraph","source_block_sha256":"bc0e563f9e7c18c838cf740db8a8b8918b734cba9720e292b24c17c921cb4e46","source_file_id":"urn:uuid:271e812b-1ccd-53ed-a402-07fe58709c8a","source_locale":"en-US","source_locator":{"end_line":600,"layout":"Standard","layout_ordinal":29,"start_line":590},"source_text":"{{HREF:1}}","source_text_sha256":"8d3664ef161aac53875065688c2459c72e7f90c4008f1f22ee9dabd438b205e8","translation_state":"source_frozen","unit_id":"urn:uuid:844e0e01-ed7b-5fae-a80a-1a53eb0650e1"}
{"edition_id":"urn:uuid:35b4350d-7202-5d47-8b85-5262d7ca441c","id":"urn:uuid:d7992092-7bab-556f-99a4-289bbc51024a","order":3,"protected_tokens":[],"record_type":"segment","rights_id":"urn:uuid:52a7c815-5b13-5f21-a52a-e7fa11238e07","schema_id":"ttna-segment-v1","schema_version":"1.0.0","semantic_slot":"paragraph","source_block_sha256":"b7377426a68ce07878bf32fa1e31dcfcb0b8430346875c0c18f56b4036f236c4","source_file_id":"urn:uuid:271e812b-1ccd-53ed-a402-07fe58709c8a","source_locale":"en-US","source_locator":{"end_line":442,"layout":"Standard","layout_ordinal":14,"start_line":435},"source_text":"Octave is developed by the GNU Project for the GNU operating system, which is most often paired with a Linux kernel. At its core, Octave is, therefore, GNU/Linux software. It runs natively on GNU/Linux machines. Linux users will find it in their distribution's software repository. Some of the other ways to access Octave are outlined below for convenience.","source_text_sha256":"e5f8c1524846e679799c3e97721e57425e92a1cc04ed9b0a6f248579c134b0ba","translation_state":"source_frozen","unit_id":"urn:uuid:bbe5e441-2af6-532c-9a0f-f3c12b3e9e40"}
